1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,806
Case studies, part two: disaster science.

2
00:00:03,806 --> 00:00:11,728
We apply the same equation to flood and heat risk in
three African cities: Lilongwe in Malawi,

3
00:00:11,728 --> 00:00:14,847
Lagos in Nigeria, and Bamako in Mali.

4
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,949
A reminder before we start.

5
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:22,956
RMTI is a research prototype: not validated, not a
medical device,

6
00:00:22,956 --> 00:00:26,598
and not for patient-care or emergency decisions.

7
00:00:26,598 --> 00:00:32,516
Everything here uses illustrative values, published
rows, or aggregate counts.

8
00:00:32,516 --> 00:00:34,337
No patient data appears.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:40,461
Here is how the inputs map to a city, using the RMTI
indicator backbone.

10
00:00:40,461 --> 00:00:44,889
Exposure is raised by hazards, climate change and
urbanisation.

11
00:00:44,889 --> 00:00:49,176
Vulnerability is raised by poverty, governance and
pollution.

12
00:00:49,176 --> 00:00:55,783
Resilience comes from risk-management strategy,
infrastructure, and health and water security.

13
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:59,408
What can each lever mean on the ground?

14
00:00:59,408 --> 00:01:03,670
These are illustrative examples, not evidence-based
effect sizes.

15
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:08,195
For flood: keep people out of the floodplain, which acts
on exposure;

16
00:01:08,195 --> 00:01:14,490
improve housing and keep services running, which acts on
vulnerability; and build early warning,

17
00:01:14,490 --> 00:01:18,687
drainage and emergency health capacity, which raises
resilience.

18
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:23,081
For heat: reduce the share of people in the hottest
neighbourhoods,

19
00:01:23,081 --> 00:01:27,606
improve housing and tackle energy poverty, and build
cooling centres,

20
00:01:27,606 --> 00:01:30,032
heat-health plans and reliable power.

21
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:38,209
Published rubric values from the disaster calculator:
Lilongwe, pluvial and river flood; Lagos,

22
00:01:38,209 --> 00:01:44,645
coastal and pluvial flood; and Bamako, a multi-hazard
case of drought, flood and heat.

23
00:01:44,645 --> 00:01:48,985
These are rubric scores, not damage or casualty
estimates.

24
00:01:50,050 --> 00:01:51,908
Each city travels the cube.

25
00:01:51,908 --> 00:01:56,793
All three combine high likelihood with high exposure and
vulnerability.

26
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:08,223
With today's resilience, all three land in tier 4,
severe: R of 0.42 for Lilongwe, 0.37 for Lagos,

27
00:02:08,223 --> 00:02:10,340
and 0.41 for Bamako.

28
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:16,492
Under scenario resilience targets, all three fall to
tier 3, high.

29
00:02:16,492 --> 00:02:20,581
These after values are targets, not observed results.

30
00:02:21,650 --> 00:02:24,991
Lever one, resilience, for Lilongwe.

31
00:02:24,991 --> 00:02:30,930
The calculator's target pathway is 0.43 to 0.53 by 2027
to 2030.

32
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:38,076
R drops below the severe threshold of 0.35 once
resilience passes about 0.38,

33
00:02:38,076 --> 00:02:42,067
and the target pathway goes well beyond it.

34
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:47,847
Lever two, exposure, shows why inputs must be audited.

35
00:02:47,847 --> 00:02:56,637
For Lagos the published rubric uses exposure 0.65, but
the population-based helper gives about 0.98.

36
00:02:56,637 --> 00:03:04,812
Same city, same equation: R would rise from 0.37 to
0.56, still severe but close to critical.

37
00:03:04,812 --> 00:03:11,053
Sensitivity like this is exactly what an auditable index
should expose.

38
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:19,148
Lever three, vulnerability, for Bamako, where drought,
flood and heat combine.

39
00:03:19,148 --> 00:03:22,130
At vulnerability 0.75, R is 0.41.

40
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:29,900
Housing quality, service continuity and support for the
poorest would move this lever.

41
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:35,141
R drops below 0.35 once vulnerability falls to about
0.64.

42
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:42,269
For Bamako, which single lever would be enough to leave
tier 4?

43
00:03:42,269 --> 00:03:49,589
Resilience at least 0.31; vulnerability at most 0.64;
exposure at most 0.73;

44
00:03:49,589 --> 00:03:52,190
or likelihood at most 0.69.

45
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:59,126
Likelihood is the hardest to move, because it is set
largely by climate.

46
00:03:59,126 --> 00:04:02,112
Realistic plans combine levers.

47
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:11,279
Side by side for Bamako, each lever moved by ten points,
from largest to smallest: vulnerability, resilience,

48
00:04:11,279 --> 00:04:13,069
likelihood and exposure.

49
00:04:13,069 --> 00:04:18,140
The grey bar is likelihood: it moves R, but a city
cannot choose it.

50
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:21,869
The teal bars are the levers a programme can pull.

51
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,352
What this shows and does not show.

52
00:04:25,352 --> 00:04:31,193
The arithmetic and the levers are RMTI's own, applied to
published rubric values.

53
00:04:31,193 --> 00:04:38,405
The after values are scenario targets, R is a relative
rubric index, and none of this is a forecast,

54
00:04:38,405 --> 00:04:41,722
an engineering assessment, or a loss estimate.

55
00:04:41,722 --> 00:04:46,410
Investment decisions need local data and professional
assessment.

56
00:04:47,450 --> 00:04:51,755
That is RMTI on flood and heat: one equation, four
levers,

57
00:04:51,755 --> 00:04:55,987
and a clear view of what each would take to leave a
tier.

58
00:04:55,987 --> 00:04:57,620
I'm Dr. Cesar Marolla.

59
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:02,445
The code is open under Apache 2.0, the demo uses no
patient data,

60
00:05:02,445 --> 00:05:05,340
and nothing here is engineering advice.
